# DIGITZ Tracker — Renewal Workflow

## A document and its cycles

A **Tracked Document** has a holder (an organisation or a person), a type, number, authority, issue and expiry dates, an optional explicit assignee, its groups and an attachment.

**Its file.** *Add document*, *Edit document* and *Renew* have an upload for the document's file (a scan or PDF). Files are stored privately: only people who may see the document can open them, with **Open file** in its panel. When the document type has **Upload required** and there is no file:
- the document is saved all the same, and a warning says the file is missing;
- its card shows a **No file** flag, and its panel says *Missing — required for this document type*;
- Home counts it under **Uploads missing**.

**AI check of the file.** Each new file is read by an AI model (OpenAI) and compared with the document: is it this document type, the holder's, with the same number and expiry date? It runs in the background a few seconds after saving, and never stops a save. The panel shows **AI check**:
- **Verified**: the file matches the record;
- **Needs review**: something differs or could not be read; the note says what, for example *Expiry on the file: 2026-01-01 (record: 2027-01-01)*. The card shows a **Check file** flag;
- **Not checked**: the check is switched off, no OpenAI key is set, or the file is not a PDF, picture or text file (or is larger than the limit);
- **Failed**: the AI service could not be reached; use **Check again**.

**Check again** (in the panel, for staff who may edit the document) checks the current file once more. The AI only advises: it never changes the document, and a *Verified* file can still be wrong. The file is sent to OpenAI for the check and is not kept there.

Each validity cycle keeps the file it was valid with. **Renew** asks for the file of the renewed document; if none is uploaded, the renewed document starts without a file (and is flagged when the type requires one) until someone uploads it with *Edit document*.

**Its type** is one of the holder's country's document types, or a type with no country (chapter 2).

**Its groups.** *Add document* and *Edit document* have one **Groups** field, shown as tags, one per dimension:
- a **dashed tag marked *auto*** is the group the document gets from its type or holder, for example "Document category · Licences & permits"; hold the pointer over it to see where it comes from;
- **+ Add group** opens one list of every group under its dimension heading, with a search box. Picking a group sets it on this document and replaces the dimension's previous group, because a document has one group per dimension;
- a group set on the document is a **solid tag with ×**; the × removes it, and the dimension goes back to its automatic group.

On a new document the automatic groups are not known until it is saved, so only the groups you add are shown. Organisation and person forms use the same field for their department-like groups; there, × means no group. The panel shows each group and where it came from: from the document type, from the holder, from the organisation (a person without a group of their own), set on this document, or *No group*.

Each validity period is a **Document Cycle**:
- **Adding** a document with an expiry date opens cycle 1. With only an issue date, the expiry date is the issue date plus the type's validity (e.g. 2 Yearly). A type without expiry has no cycles.
- **Correcting** the expiry date (*Edit document*) corrects the open cycle; it is not a renewal.
- **Start renewal**: the cycle becomes *Under renewal*.
- **Renew**, with the new expiry date (proposed as the current expiry plus the type's validity) and the renewal date:
  - the cycle closes as *Renewed*;
  - the document takes the new dates;
  - cycle 2 opens, with its people worked out afresh.
- **Discontinue**: the cycle closes and the document stops being tracked.

A cycle also keeps:
- the **assignee** and **managers** who handled it;
- its **reminders** sent;
- its **payment status** (Unpaid, Paid, Settled) and **payment follow-ups**.

A document has only one open cycle at a time. A closed cycle never changes again.
